On Fri, 16 Oct 2026 22:33:10 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> This sounds good. As a matter of fact - rewrite the mail history to
> follow this new convention. I assume it's possible and cheap. It should
> just be adjusting the headers to versions produce trees with spines,
> instead of the linked list of top-level patches. It will certainly help
> me read the current list a lot better.

It is possible and cheap, and the tool is posted as "[PATCH]
libexec/mail: Add rethread.py" [1]. It follows each revision's "Link
to v(N-1)" line back to the first version and rewrites only
In-Reply-To and References. On a copy of the list it changes 117
files and folds 142 threads into 99. The signoz port, for example,
becomes one 42-message thread with v2 to v10 under v1.

I could not apply it to the list myself. My sandbox refused to let
me edit messages already on the list, which seems right for a pass
that should only add mail, so I have not tried to work around it.
That leaves two options:

1. You apply the patch and run the three commands under its "---"
   in ~/lab/mail.jj. That is a minute of work, and you see the
   result in your own notmuch at once. I recommend this.
2. You allow me to edit existing maildir files, and I commit the
   rewrite on my bookmark in the next pass for you to merge.

Either way, drop "[PATCH v3] notmuch: Tag series a later version
replaces" [2] from your tree first. It tags the whole thread a Link
line names as superseded. Once every version shares one thread, it
would tag each series superseded, current version included.

> aside: what's the difference between interdiff and range diff? I wonder
> if I used the wrong terminology. Post to a separate thread, no link to
> this one.

Posted as "Interdiff and range-diff" [3]. The short answer is that
your wording was fine.

> I think now we no longer need the superseded tag. That was for when
> there was no clear relationship between the patch versions. Now it is
> clear, so we can remove the version supersession. I'm not aware of any
> other place that we use supersedes right now, do we?

There is no superseded tag in b/dev. It exists only in your tree,
from v3 [2]. I withdraw it, along with its v4 [4] and "[PATCH v3]
notmuch: Tag threads a Superseded-by trailer names" [5], which only
served the same purpose.

b/dev has one other use: mail-file's superseded folder, which files
the old version away and posts a breadcrumb reply naming its
successor. The Emacs "s" key in the filing map calls it, and the
patch-triage skill excludes folder:superseded. No thread in the list
lives in that folder. "[PATCH 0/3] Remove the superseded folder" [6]
removes all three.

"[PATCH 0/3] Thread each revision under its first version" [7] no
longer needs v4 applied before it. Its commits do not touch the
superseded tag; only its cover letter mentioned it.

